rules.rule:
A test that cannot fail is worse than no test, because it is read as coverage.

Reject each of these outright, with a line reference:
- an assertion on a constant, or on a string the test itself just constructed;
- a source-text scan (`code.contains("class")`) standing in for a behavioural property;
- an early-return guard (`if (plan.found()) return;`) that lets the test pass while asserting nothing;
- an assertion that pins the CURRENT BUGGY STRING, so it goes green precisely because the defect exists -- the canonical case being an assertion that a description names a tool which does not exist;
- an expected value computed by calling the production method under test (a mirror assertion), rather than an independently derived number;
- a bound that equals the value under test, so the test fails intermittently and is then loosened instead of fixed;
- a test whose only assertion is that a count is greater than zero, or that a list is empty.

A new test must be mutation-checked before it is claimed: restore the pre-change code and show it goes red. State in the commit message that this was done. 'Tests pass' is not evidence that they bite.
